---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const title = "VolEq privacy policy | Patrik Dóczy";
const description =
  "How VolEq processes captured audio locally, uses offline speech analysis, and contacts GitHub only for optional update checks and release pages.";
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description} pathname="/privacy/voleq/">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/privacy/"
          >Privacy</a
        ><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">VolEq</span>
      </nav>
      <p class="eyebrow">Product privacy policy</p>
      <h1>VolEq privacy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Local audio processing, no accounts, and no usage telemetry.
      </p>
      <p class="updated">Last updated: 10 August 2026</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell content-grid">
      <aside>
        <p class="eyebrow">VolEq</p>
        <p><a href="/projects/voleq/">Product overview</a></p>
        <p><a href="/support/voleq/">Support</a></p>
        <p><a href="/privacy/">Website privacy</a></p>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <div class="note">
          <p>
            This HTML rendering is provided for convenient public access. The
            repository policy is authoritative.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a
              href="https://github.com/DPatrikI/voleq-community/blob/master/PRIVACY.md"
              >Read the canonical VolEq privacy policy</a
            >
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>Audio stays local</h2>
        <p>
          VolEq processes captured audio locally and in memory. Captured audio
          is not saved, uploaded, or used for telemetry.
        </p>
        <p>
          When speech-aware leveling is enabled, bundled RNNoise states analyze
          and mildly denoise speech locally. Turning speech awareness off skips
          RNNoise and speech analysis entirely. Apple’s offline SoundAnalysis
          framework supplies the slower speech-versus-music decision. There are
          no model downloads or online speech-processing services.
        </p>

        <h2>Permission and audio safety</h2>
        <p>
          macOS controls System Audio Recording permission. VolEq explains its
          local-only audio use before the real processing pipeline causes macOS
          to present its permission prompt. VolEq does not infer permission from
          silence or run a separate permission probe.
        </p>
        <p>
          An ordinary Core Audio startup failure leaves VolEq non-running and
          begins complete graph teardown. If Core Audio refuses to stop or
          destroy a resource, VolEq retains ownership, requires Quit, and does
          not claim that the original audio path was restored. The
          always-visible <strong>No sound?</strong> help repeats the privacy guarantee
          and opens the relevant System Settings page.
        </p>

        <h2>Application information</h2>
        <p>
          The application selector displays process information that macOS
          exposes for processes currently producing audio. VolEq does not
          intentionally persist that process list.
        </p>

        <h2>Accounts and analytics</h2>
        <p>
          VolEq creates no user accounts and includes no analytics. It retains
          no user dataset that a user could ask Patrik to delete.
        </p>

        <h2>Optional update checks</h2>
        <p>
          When a user chooses <strong>Check for Updates</strong>, or explicitly
          enables automatic checks, VolEq makes an unauthenticated request to <code
            >api.github.com</code
          > for the latest published VolEq Community release. Automatic checks run
          at most once in a rolling 24-hour period and only while VolEq is running.
          Opening <strong>View Release</strong> contacts
          <code>github.com</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          GitHub receives ordinary network metadata such as an IP address during
          update checks or when a release page is opened. VolEq sends no
          captured audio, account identifier, user identifier, or usage
          telemetry to GitHub. It stores no GitHub response body, cookie, or
          credential. The setting can be changed in VolEq Settings.
        </p>

        <h2>Policy changes</h2>
        <p>
          The canonical policy must be updated before VolEq adds telemetry,
          additional networking, persistent recordings, crash reporting, or
          other data collection.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
